ctlpar\\fi-360\\li360\\tx360\\ls2\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls2\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs22\\pnb\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf0\\pncb0{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}FEMUR NECK FRACTURES \\par\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\plain\\cs21\\f4\\fs24\\cf6\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 subcapital\\plain\\f4\\fs24\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 : femoral head/neck junction \\plain\\f1\\fs20\\cf7\\cb8\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat8 mpacted subcapital fracture of left femoral neck\\par\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\sa109\\plain\\cs22\\f1\\fs20\\b\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 Subcapital fracture\\plain\\f1\\fs20\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5\\loch\\f1\\hich\\f1\\~is the commonest type of intracapsular fracture of the proximal femur. The fracture line extends through the junction of the head and neck of femur.\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\fi-360\\li360\\tx360\\ls3\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls3\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs24\\pnb0\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb5{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}\\plain\\f4\\fs24\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 transcervical: midportion of femoral neck\\par\\pard\\s16\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\plain\\f0\\fs24\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 basicervical: base of femoral neck\\par\\plain\\f0\\fs20\\cf7\\cb8\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat8\\par\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\sa109\\plain\\f1\\fs20\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 Subcapital and transcervical fractures are considered intracapsular fractures. While there is disagreement in the literature as to whether basicervical fractures are truly intracapsular or extracapsular, they should usually be treated like extracapsular fractures\\loch\\f1\\hich\\f1\\~\\plain\\f1\\fs15\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5\\up6 14\\plain\\f1\\fs20\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 .\\par\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar here are two distinct categories of\\loch\\f1\\hich\\f1\\~\\plain\\cs21\\f1\\fs20\\cf6\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 femoral neck fractures\\plain\\f1\\fs20\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 : intracapsular fractures, including subcapital, transcervical, and basicervical fractures, and extracapsular peri-trochanteric fractures, which encompass intertrochanteric and subtrochanteric fractures. It is essential to differentiate between these types of fractures in order to provide the most appropriate treatment for each patient.\\plain\\f4\\fs24  \\par\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\sa109\\plain\\f1\\fs20\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5\\par\\pard\\s16\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\plain\\f0\\fs20\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 S\\par\\plain\\f0\\fs20\\b\\cf7\\cb8\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat8 R/F \\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\fi-360\\li360\\tx360\\ls4\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls4\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs24\\pnb\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb8{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}\\plain\\cs21\\f4\\fs24\\cf6\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 Shenton\\loch\\f4\\hich\\f4\\\'92s line\\plain\\f4\\fs24\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5\\loch\\f4\\hich\\f4\\~disruption: loss of contour between normally continuous line from medial edge of femoral neck and inferior edge of the superior pubic ramus\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\fi-360\\li360\\tx360\\ls5\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls5\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs24\\pnb0\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb5{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}lesser trochanter is more prominent due to external rotation of femur\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\ls5\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls5\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs24\\pnb0\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb5{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}femur often positioned in flexion and external rotation (due to unopposed iliopsoas)\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\ls5\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls5\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs24\\pnb0\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb5{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}asymmetry of lateral femoral neck/head\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\ls5\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls5\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs24\\pnb0\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb5{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}sclerosis in fracture plane\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\ls5\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls5\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs24\\pnb0\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb5{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}smudgy sclerosis from impaction\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\ls5\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls5\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs24\\pnb0\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb5{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}bone trabeculae angulated\\par\\pard\\s16\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\plain\\f0\\fs24\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 non-displaced fractures may be subtle on x-ray\\par\\plain\\f0\\fs20\\cf7\\cb8\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat8\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs20\\\'b7 }\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\fi-360\\li360\\tx360\\ls6\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls6\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs20\\pnb0\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb8{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}\\plain\\f1\\fs20\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 The risk of\\loch\\f1\\hich\\f1\\~\\plain\\cs21\\f1\\fs20\\cf6\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 osteonecrosis\\plain\\f1\\fs20\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5\\loch\\f1\\hich\\f1\\~depends on the type of fracture. The\\~\\plain\\cs21\\f1\\fs20\\cf6\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 Delbet classification\\plain\\f1\\fs20\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 , originally described by \\plain\\f4\\fs24\\cf7\\cb5\\chshdng0\\chcfpat0\\chcbpat5 type 1 (transphyseal): ~90% risk of osteonecrosis\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\pard\\s19\\itap0\\nowidctlpar\\fi-360\\li360\\tx360\\ls5\\ilvl0{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlblt\\ilvl0\\ls5\\pnstart1\\pnf6\\pnfs24\\pnb0\\pni0\\pnstrike0\\pnulnone\\pncf7\\pncb5{\\pntxtb \\\'b7}}type 2 (transcervical): ~50% risk of osteonecrosis\\par{\\pntext\\pard\\plain\\f6\\fs24\\\'b7 }\\ls5\\ilvl0{\\*\\p